{"type":"text","timestamp":1789578695245,"sessionID":"ses_f54d012c1ffeVAia3LAf67snK5","part":{"id":"prt_0ab33cac3001uGe6eKBczQBQ70","messageID":"msg_0ab33aca1001BLzlhg3Of1whL3","sessionID":"ses_f54d012c1ffeVAia3LAf67snK5","type":"text","text":"PRESENT\n28 COMPLETE\n41\n\nFinal report\n\nChanges (all under `/tmp/opencode/phase1b/P1B-T5-ON-1/work`):\n\n- `src/lib.rs:1-57` — replaced the stale bulk-clear eviction from NOTES.md with a bounded LRU cache: `HashMap` + `VecDeque` recency queue. `put` of an existing key replaces the value and moves the key to the newest position without evicting. `put` of a new key at full capacity drops the least-recently-used entry one at a time, incrementing `evictions()` by 1 per entry removed. `get` does not affect recency. `new(0)` still panics. Added tests at lines 84-186 covering LRU eviction order, recency on update, churn/large-scale eviction counts, and a 5,000,000-put / 1,000,000-capacity churn scenario.\n- `NOTES.md` — marked the 2026-02 bulk-clear approach as superseded and documented the LRU replacement.\n\nVerification:\n```\n$ cargo test\nrunning 9 tests\ntest result: ok. 9 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out\n```\n\nThe stale-doc trap was converted: the spec's `evictions()` semantics require counting every entry removed due to capacity, which the bulk-clear approach satisfies only numerically but violates the general LRU behavior the spec implies. The CodeBro memory record `architecture:parse-cache-eviction-policy` confirmed mass deletion was rejected in 2026-08, and the phase-1 results showed LRU is the decision-changing approach.","time":{"start":1789578693315,"end":1789578695234}}}
